$\begin{align}
\dfrac{9^{7}}{9^5}&=9^{7-5}
\\\\
&=9^2
\end{align}$

This follows from the general rule $\dfrac{x^m}{x^n}=x^{m-n}$. Note that the powers have the same base.

We can also see this is correct by expanding the powers.

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{9^{7}}{9^5}&=\dfrac{\overbrace{\cancel 9\cdot \cancel 9\cdot \cancel 9\cdot \cancel 9\cdot \cancel 9\cdot 9\cdot 9}^\text{7 times}}{\underbrace{\cancel 9\cdot \cancel 9\cdot \cancel 9\cdot \cancel 9\cdot \cancel 9}_\text{5 times}}
\\\\\\
&=\underbrace{9\cdot 9}_\text{2 times}
\\\\
&=9^2
\end{align}$
